  "summary": "Luxurious silk kaftans made in Bursa for Ottoman court officials and royalty.",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate patterns, rich colors, and gold thread embroidery; these kaftans reflected the wearer's status and the empire's wealth. They were produced in Bursa's renowned silk workshops, which supplied the Ottoman court with the finest textiles.",
  "culturalContext": "Bursa's court kaftans — Topkapı Palace's 2,500-piece kaftan collection (14th-19th c.) — wore Ottoman silk power: çatma velvet, kemha brocade and seraser gold-cloth woven in Bursa's looms, cut as straight-panel sleeved robes with no waist seam, lined in sable and fox for sultans. Süleyman the Magnificent's kaftans set European fashion envy; the transfer-weave inscriptions ( names woven into cloth) made garments documents, and Istanbul museum shops still sell Bursa-woven kaftan replicas.",
